tinyEnv — build and run the optional desktop AI workbench

Run from the repository root. Rust and Docker are not required for the workbench.
Requires Git, CMake >=3.24, Ninja, Python >=3.9 and a C++20 compiler.
The FLTK desktop host also needs the distribution's FLTK development package.

Build and start the FLTK workbench on a Linux desktop:
./build.sh workbench build release --backend fltk --build-dir build/workbench-fltk-release
./build/workbench-fltk-release/tinyenv-workbench-fltk --root /absolute/path/to/project

Or omit --root for ~/tinyEnv/casual-chats (created on first use):
./build/workbench-fltk-release/tinyenv-workbench-fltk

Use release for optimized desktop operation and dev for debugging. Changing the
preset, backend or toolchain requires a fresh build directory.
Run the focused offline development checks without a display or network:
./build.sh workbench test dev --headless --build-dir build/workbench-checks

Framebuffers use the existing SDL2 display/input host: install libsdl2-dev,
then select --backend framebuffer and a fresh
build/workbench-framebuffer-release tree.
Rev uses --backend rev and a fresh build/workbench-rev-release tree; its existing
prepared module toolchain is required. Retained sources are used without downloads.

Chats use the .chat.md suffix; other Markdown files remain ordinary documents.
Their companion state holds each chat's composer draft, selectors, AI settings,
pseudocode, tabs and terminal directory. Chat changes autosave after a 300 ms
quiet period and flush on switch/close; external changes cause a conflict and
keep the current buffer available. Ordinary file editors retain manual Save.
The global Tutorial and Developer checkboxes start off and persist across
projects and restarts. Developer enables editing the otherwise read-only chat
history. Ctrl+Z undoes, Ctrl+Y or Ctrl+Shift+Z redoes in the focused composer,
flow source, Developer history or file editor, and
Ctrl+Enter sends once from the composer. Copy text file… copies saved UTF-8 bytes
to an unused project-relative path, including valid companion state for chats.
For an autosave conflict, the file actions offer Copy retained chat and draft…
and confirmed Discard retained changes…; the original buffer stays available
until explicitly discarded.
Right-click a Running Tasks row for its targeted Cancel task or Stop terminal.
Retry beside Cancel and Send uses the recorded prompt, mode and pre-submission history with current
visible AI/Selector settings, preserves the unsent text/images and prior attempts,
and survives restart. Image Retry resends the original managed snapshots. It
never infers a retry from chat Markdown or reruns automatically. Editing the
original message or its preceding history invalidates Retry. Send/Retry use visible AI fields without Apply; a missing or
invalid model opens AI, preserves the draft and starts no task.
The bottom-bar Sidebar checkbox starts on and persists across projects/restarts.
It stays reachable when the right pane is hidden; hiding expands the center and
showing restores the selected right tab and its fields/scroll position.
Send/Retry validation needing AI settings reveals the sidebar at AI's controls.
Selector's Code checkbox starts off and is saved independently per chat.
The selector.calculations boolean and calculate tool name remain compatible.
It permits a bounded numeric AST interpreter during Chat/Agentic replies:
arithmetic/math, numeric lists, scalar indexed writes, read-only slices,
nonrecursive functions, bounded loops and integer bit operations in Python-shaped
syntax. It can support information calculations, basic numeric vision, signal
filters and QR algorithms from scratch within its budgets; no QR library is loaded.
Optional data accepts only bounded finite numeric/boolean nested lists. Pixels
must already be explicit numeric arrays; there is no PNG/JPEG decoding, file or
metadata access, or automatic access to composer attachments. Program quotes and
backslashes are rejected even in comments; there are no strings or escape decoders.
It has no imports/files/network/shell or general Python execution, and is not an
OS sandbox. Each call is capped at 32 KiB ASCII source, 2,000,000 operations,
200,000 total loop iterations, 65,536 items per list/tuple and 64 KiB JSON output,
with additional syntax, storage and numeric limits in docs/workbench.md.
It never executes through Scribble/evaluation or from a displayed code fence alone.
Attach image beside the composer asks for one absolute or project-relative
PNG/JPEG path; selecting an image in Files also offers Attach image to message.
Pending images show a filename/byte count and Remove. Limits are four images,
4 MiB each and 8 MiB raw total. Each is copied to an immutable SHA-256 base64
snapshot under project .tinyenv/images; state holds bounded metadata. Pending
images survive chat switching/restart; Send clears submitted text/images after a
successful launch, and Retry preserves the current draft. Reattach an earlier
image for a later follow-up; ordinary history does not replay image bytes. Chat
accepts image-only messages; Agentic still requires its current closed flow fence
and its smaller conservative run budget also counts encoded images. Select an
image-capable model explicitly; attaching makes no provider request. Snapshots
are plaintext and retained without automatic cleanup; moving a chat to another
project needs its state and project .tinyenv/images too. Missing/modified snapshots
refuse Send/Retry. OpenAI Responses, OpenRouter and Ollama image input share the
16 MiB default provider input limit with text/history/context; the native/runtime
request envelope is also 16 MiB. Protocol references are in docs/workbench.md.
Chat and agent writer replies stream into a bounded, periodically autosaved
preview. Validated visible fragments are forwarded without waiting for another
provider event. The desktop batches rapid fragments with a 16 ms target interval;
provider transport, native polling and redraws still apply. This interval does
not guarantee a frame rate or provider latency.
Checked autosaves remain synchronous; large chats can pause updates while saving.
Streaming stays bounded to 4,096 delta frames within an 8 MiB envelope. The final
answer replaces its preview once; cancellation or failure retains valid partial
text as incomplete. Critiques and internal tool envelopes are not answer
previews. A stationary ring in the main transcript corner marks a pending reply.
A thin graphical divider marks the end of saved nonblank main/split transcripts
when that chat has no active Chat/Agentic reply. It has no text or timer, including
in reopened chats and saved incomplete results. Successful Send/Retry launch
hides it; a rejected launch retains it. Unsaved history or a save conflict hides
it. The divider stays out of chat Markdown, state and provider context.
Views follow the latest text/table using the reachable scroll end as wrapped
content and scroll ranges settle, including retained incomplete output. Range
growth or end clamping does not count as scrolling up. Scrolling up pauses
following; scrolling back to the reachable end resumes it. Send/Retry resume
the main view, while paused split readers retain their position through new
output and view switches.
Normal/split history passively formats headings, paragraphs, lists, up to
eight quote layers, quoted fenced code, simple standalone one-line strong text
and thematic rules, plus a restricted native Mermaid flowchart subset. Valid
pipe tables and exact closed markdown fences containing only one table display
native cells with drawn rules and bold headers. Selected cell text copies
individually. Cells wrap; full rows use the outer transcript scroll, with
horizontal scrolling in narrow lanes. Tables have at most eight columns, 32
data rows and 128 displayed ASCII bytes per cell; input and retained ASCII
fallback must each fit the 8 KiB block budget. Each view graphically renders
the newest fitting tables within 384 cells and 2,097,152 bitmap device pixels;
others keep selectable ASCII fallback. Unsupported candidates and other fenced
code remain literal. Unfinished streamed rows can temporarily switch to
literal text; fenced tables wait for closure. Mixed inline emphasis/code and
Markdown images remain literal; there is no resource fetching or image preview
decoder.
The flowchart subset accepts bounded ASCII label punctuation and plain
whole-line comments; circle/stadium syntax uses a rounded-box approximation.
Simple node fill/stroke/width declarations are validated and discarded, keeping
the fixed native palette; supported values and limits are in docs/workbench.md.
Configuration directives and unsupported syntax remain literal code.
Only exact message title blocks have widget borders; body paragraphs, lists and
code remain borderless and selectable. User titles/bodies appear on
the right and assistant titles/bodies on the left in main, split and streaming
views.
Non-ASCII preview text uses fixed, nonrecursive [U+XXXX] escapes (malformed
bytes [0xXX]); saved source is unchanged. Developer edits the original Markdown
and pauses while the chat's reply streams. Preview bounds and diagram grammar
are documented in docs/workbench.md.
AI requests require explicit provider configuration. Masked Provider API key
entry has explicit Save key/Remove saved key actions for OpenRouter and OpenAI.
Keys are plaintext in user config credentials/{openrouter,openai}.key, outside
chat state and preferences, with POSIX directory mode 0700 and file mode 0600.
A saved key overrides existing environment/private configuration; Remove
restores that fallback. Unsupported storage hosts retain environment authentication, and Rev
masked entry is unavailable. Ollama is local and unauthenticated.
Automated checks use only local fixtures; live provider and curated-search checks
are manual and tightly bounded.
AI Apply remembers defaults across projects and restarts. A fresh unconfigured
chat has a blank model unless the user has saved defaults. Existing settings
are preserved. Builder annotation/evaluation model fields also start blank;
reviewed profiles and examples remain explicit choices.
Open Build Scribble / Ref… in Selector, Files or a file/folder's right-click
actions after opening a chat. It opens the full Scribble builder workspace with
Setup, Tasks and Results tabs. Selector initially supplies the project root;
Files/actions initially supply the selected path or current directory. Reentry
restores the chat's saved setup, tab and selection. Exit Scribble builder returns
to the normal workbench; running work continues and drafts/setup/tasks remain.
Escape neither leaves builder mode nor cancels work. New task explicitly resets
setup while retaining previous records and initially seeds its source from Files
or the current browsed directory. Each chat retains up to 32 task records
and runs one worker at a time; there is no automatic queue or launch on restart.
Running records become interrupted after application shutdown/restart.
In Tasks, Pause stops a Scribble build and Resume Scribble explicitly continues
its saved request on supported POSIX hosts. Resume verifies the original root,
chat, sources, settings and checkpoint; it refuses changed or missing data
without starting a fresh build. An interrupted unsaved provider call may repeat.
Start saved Scribble explicitly starts a retained task whose launch did not
start. Failure before the first manifest may need New task with a fresh output.
Remove record is available only for nonrunning records and preserves generated
outputs/checkpoints. Results offers View generated Scribble, Use as Scribble and
Use as Ref; the Use actions add and enable the output DIRECTORY in Selector.
The builder starts with Local source index · no AI or network. Build local index
preserves source anchors and local keywords without models, credentials or
network requests. Explicitly select AI annotations · OpenRouter for AI settings;
enter an ID or choose a compatible configured model. Built-in model examples
appear only with Tutorial and are not defaults or availability claims. OpenAI
models cannot build or evaluate Scribbles. Evaluation is a separate opt-in with
an explicit model and Evaluate existing action, even from local mode; it uses
the saved index/questions without research. Cancel evaluation stops it; Retry
evaluation explicitly starts a new task with new provider calls. Evaluation has
no resumable pair checkpoint. AI Build can opt into Limited research for Build
using Selector's research source. Resume reuses saved research without new
searches. Ordinary chat context and Tools selections are not automatically added
as builder inputs.
Use a fresh output directory inside the project: scribbles.md, index.json and
evidence.json are never overwritten by a new build. POSIX builds retain a hidden
sibling .tinyenv-scribble-<digest>.checkpoint with immutable source/config/provider
bindings and incremental completed annotations. Resume reuses only durably saved
chunks and can finish its own verified partial final publication. Checkpoints
remain after success or Remove record. Windows fresh builds remain available
without checkpoints, while Resume is unavailable there; macOS desktop workers
remain unavailable. The configured call budget must fit the planned build and
is fixed for Resume. It caps each explicit Build/Resume invocation, not lifetime
attempts; usage counts new work and reports reused research/annotations separately.
Add and enable the output DIRECTORY in Selector's Scribbles or Refs list to use
the verified index; the Markdown file alone loads raw text. Scribble progress
streams as typed phase/chunk updates in the builder workspace, separately from
chat answer text; final result metadata remains authoritative. Runtime requests
may set scribble.resume:true (strict JSON boolean) and stream:true explicitly;
evaluation remains buffered. Legacy single-JSON workers remain accepted.
Blank routes for custom OpenRouter models use deepinfra,together,mistral;
known profiles retain their exact tags and explicit allowlists remain in effect.
Requests use data_collection:deny, allow_fallbacks:false and require_parameters:true
within configured price ceilings. Corporate U.S./France selection is not a
data-residency guarantee. Keep OpenRouter's own logging/data-use opt-ins off;
the workbench does not alter account settings. Reviewed policies and limits are
in docs/workbench.md. User chat searches can
use general web sources; the curated source limits are for qualification.
Blank generation controls and endpoints use the provider's defaults. Refresh models explicitly
loads the selected provider's catalog; Add/Remove maintains a local configured list.
Filter available models matches loaded model IDs/names locally; Clear restores
the full catalog. Filtering makes no request or saved-model change.
Pseudocode snippets preview before explicit insertion; built-in examples require
Tutorial. Flow text, graph edits and snippets modify only the current composer's
fenced flow. Agentic Send requires exactly one closed pseudocode/tinyenv/flow
fence in the submitted message; history or saved pseudocode cannot supply it.
The flow canvas mouse wheel zooms around the pointer from 0.2 to 2 times scale,
up to zoom in and down to zoom out, without changing the workflow.
There is no Zero retention control: legacy zdr settings are ignored. OpenAI
uses store:false; Ollama sends client-managed history with no retention/store
option, and keep_alive controls loaded-model memory.
The AI pane does not expose account-management controls. OpenRouter documents no
request-only prompt-injection block using a normal inference key; its persistent
guardrail API requires a separate management key. See docs/workbench.md.

Usage, provider policy and qualification limits: docs/workbench.md.
